 "meta_desc": "Class 7 A Peek Beyond the Point worksheet (Ganita Prakash Part 1, Chapter 3): decimals, tenths and hundredths, place value, comparing, unit conversions and adding decimals. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "0.1 = one tenth, 0.01 = one hundredth",
 "key_sub": "Line up the decimal points to add or subtract.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>After the decimal point come the <b>tenths</b>, then the <b>hundredths</b>: 3.47 = 3 + 4/10 + 7/100.</p><p>To <b>compare</b> decimals, compare the whole parts, then the tenths, then the hundredths: 0.3 = 0.30 is greater than 0.25.</p><p>Units: 100 cm = 1 m, 1,000 g = 1 kg, 100 paise = ₹1. To add or subtract, line up the points.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Which is greater: 0.4 or 0.38?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>0.4 = 0.40</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>40 hundredths > 38 hundredths</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 0.4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 12.5 + 3.75.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>12.50 + 3.75</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 16.25</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Thinking 0.25 > 0.3 because 25 > 3.</li><li>Lining up the last digits instead of the decimal points.</li><li>Writing 5 cm as 0.5 m (it is 0.05 m).</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does each digit after the decimal point mean?",
   "a": "The first is tenths, the second hundredths, the third thousandths."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you compare decimals?",
   "a": "Compare the whole numbers, then the tenths, then the hundredths. Writing them with the same number of decimal places helps."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you add decimals?",
   "a": "Write them with the decimal points in a column and add as with whole numbers."
  }
